  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Monday)\nIssued at 1245 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows shortwave lifting north of the forecast\narea along with another stout shortwave trough ejecting into the \ncentral and southern Plains. Surface low is centered over ND/SD/MN\nborder with cold front stretching southward into western KS and a\nsecond surface low over west-central KS. Northwest winds continue\nacross the forecast area. Gusts have started to come up over the \npast hour with 50-55 mph gusts across Meade and Pennington counties.\nExpect wind gusts to increase through the afternoon with occasional\ngusts to 60 mph through the evening hours across Fall River and \nportions of western SD plains where a High Wind Warning remains in\neffect. Elsewhere, gusts 40 to 50 mph are expected through the \nevening hours.\n\nPrecipitation has increased across the northern Black Hills over the \npast few hours given upslope enhancement. The models have been too \nquick with the transition from rain to snow across the Hills compared\nto observations, leading to unrealistic snow accumulation that \nhas yet to materialize. Starting to see the transition to all snow\nnow across the higher elevations of the Black Hills, which will \ncontinue through 22z. Melting and timing of transition to all \nsnow introduce considerable uncertainty regarding final amounts, \nespecially below 5500 feet. Have issued a Winter Weather Advisory \nfor the Wyoming and northern Black Hills where 4 to 6 inches of \nsnow is possible by 12z Tuesday. Heaviest snowfall will be limited\nto areas above 5500 feet, with locally higher amounts possible. \nLesser amounts (~an inch or less) for lower elevations. Snow will \nend early Tuesday morning with light rain lingering across south- \ncentral SD into the late morning/early afternoon.\n\nUpper trough forecast to push through the Central Plains Wednesday \nwith light rain possible across south-central SD. Otherwise, \ngenerally dry weather expected through the week with seasonable \ntemperatures. Brisk northwest flow next Saturday as clipper drops \nout of Canada with wind gusts > 40 mph possible and precipitation \nnortheast of the area.",
